@@s001 Random numbers without effects
@@s002 dato:state (seed),ora:call in progress,ok:number produced,no:state readable again
@@s003 A <b>pure</b> generator: an immutable state, <code>SimpleRNG(42)</code>. 42 is the seed, the initial "seed".
@@s004 <code>r0.nextInt</code> changes nothing: it <b>returns</b> a pair, the number <b>16159453</b> and a new state <code>r1</code>.
@@s005 For the next number one calls <code>nextInt</code> on the <b>new</b> state <code>r1</code>: <b>−1281479697</b> comes out and the state <code>r2</code>. The state must be passed by hand from one call to the next.
@@s006 And if one calls <code>r0.nextInt</code> again? Again <b>16159453</b> comes out and again <code>r1</code>. Same input, same output: the function is <b>referentially transparent</b>. With <code>scala.util.Random</code> instead two equal calls give different numbers.
@@s007 no:hidden state that changes,dato:explicit state,ok:same input = same output
@@s008 dato:state,ora:generator,ok:result
@@s009 no:error,ok:fix
@@s010 Random numbers without effects
@@s011 Week 5, exercises 1–4 (chapter 6 of the book). An ordinary random number generator has a hidden state that changes at every call: it is not pure. The functional solution is to make the state <b>explicit</b>: each call receives the state and returns a new one. It is the first step towards the <a href="s05-state.html">State monad</a>.
@@s012 The problem: a hidden state
@@s013 Why rollDie is not a pure function
@@s014 
@@s015 Same expression, different results: it depends on <code>rng</code>, an external state that changes at every call (and that anyone can change).
@@s016 I cannot replace an expression with its value: <b>referential transparency</b> is missing (week 1). Testing and reasoning become difficult.
@@s017 
@@s018 The function changes nothing: it also returns the next state, and whoever uses it passes it to the next call.
@@s019 Hidden var against explicit state
@@s020 RNG and SimpleRNG
@@s021 From the exercise file
@@s022 A <code>trait</code> (an interface) with a single method. The return type is a <b>pair</b>: the number and the generator to use the next time.
@@s023 The <code>case class</code> is immutable: the seed is not changed, a new <code>SimpleRNG</code> is created.
@@s024 A linear congruential generator (the same as <code>java.util.Random</code>): it multiplies, adds, and with <code>&amp;</code> (bitwise AND) keeps only 48 bits. <code>L</code> means Long.
@@s025 <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> shifts the bits to the right filling with zeros; <code>.toInt</code> keeps 32 bits, so the number can be negative. The number is returned with the new state.
@@s026 Passing the state by hand
@@s027 All the numbers were obtained by running the code (Scala 3.8.4).
@@s028 Exercises 1–4: generators by hand
@@s029 Solutions (the 27 RNG tests of the course pass)
@@s030 Ex. 1. The awkward case: <code>Int.MinValue</code> = −2147483648 has no opposite among Ints (the maximum is 2147483647). With <code>-(i + 1)</code> every negative goes into a non-negative: −1 → 0, MinValue → 2147483647. With <code>r1</code> of the example: −1281479697 → 1281479696.
@@s031 Ex. 2. One divides by MaxValue + 1, not by MaxValue: so 1.0 never comes out (the exercise asks for [0, 1)). From <code>SimpleRNG(42)</code>: 0.0075…
@@s032 Ex. 3. The state is passed like a baton: <code>rng</code> to the first generator, <code>r1</code> to the second, <code>r2</code> to whoever comes after. Using <code>rng</code> twice would give numbers that are not independent.
@@s033 Reuse: a single generator, then I swap the order of the pair.
@@s034 Ex. 4. Recursion: a number with the current state, the other <code>size − 1</code> with the new state. <code>ints(3)(SimpleRNG(42))</code> = List(16159453, −1281479697, −340305902).
@@s035 Doubt: does Int.MaxValue.toDouble + 1 overflow?
@@s036 No. The <code>.toDouble</code> is run <b>before</b> the <code>+</code>: the +1 is done by a Double, which goes up to about 1.8 × 10³⁰⁸. It overflows only if the +1 is done by an Int, that is if you move the bracket: <code>(Int.MaxValue + 1).toDouble</code>.
@@s037 Where the bracket is decides who does the +1
@@s038 dato:Int,ora:conversion,ok:correct,no:overflow
@@s039 The largest Int: 2³¹ − 1.
@@s040 First one converts: from here on it is a Double.
@@s041 The +1 between Doubles: no problem. It is the divisor of <code>double</code>.
@@s042 Without conversion the +1 is done by an Int: it exceeds the maximum and starts again from the minimum (overflow).
@@s043 Converting afterwards does not save: the damage is already done. With this divisor <code>double</code> would give negative numbers (2147483647 / −2147483648.0 = −0.99999…).
@@s044 Doubt: is the final state of ints the same as the first recursion's?
@@s045 Each call has <b>its own</b> variables <code>r1</code> and <code>r2</code> — same names, different boxes — but the value that comes out is the same at all levels: the state born after the <b>last</b> number drawn, which climbs back up.
@@s046 It must be the last: whoever calls <code>ints(3)</code> and then wants other numbers must restart from after the three already used. Returning <code>r1</code> would make the second number come out again.
@@s047 The pattern that repeats
@@s048 All these functions have the form <span class="m">RNG =&gt; (A, RNG)</span>, and all share the same tedium: take the state, call, extract the pair, pass the new state. The next page gives a name to this form, <code>Rand[A]</code>, and hides it: <a href="s05-rand.html">Rand[A]: composable generators</a>.
@@s049 The state as the baton of a relay race
